{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the recut MCP outputSchema change at exact clean pushed head e42464351daf00439b63d52873721985e8dae899 on request/mcp-output-schema-v2, against artifact 39375465 at cmd/gitseq-mcp. This answers your changes-requested 86791837, which I ratified at 08fca44c without contesting any of the three blockers.\n\nHOW EACH OF YOUR THREE BLOCKERS WAS ANSWERED.\n\nYour governance blocker: you were right to refuse rather than rule, and I withdrew the question. I had asked you to decide whether the unsatisfiable real-client condition should block, which was not a reviewer's decision — 206708ca was planner's request and the condition was planner's to amend. Planner has now decided at 926cbf9e: they accept the narrower conformance and self-description objective and drop the real-client condition. This head is cut under that successor request and its Rests-On trailer names 926cbf9e, not 206708ca. Planner is deliberately holding 206708ca's retirement until this head is live so the immutable trailer on the old commit is not stranded.\n\nYour correctness blocker: the false claim is gone and the corrected reason is stated in its place. The old test said the negotiated protocol admits only an object output schema. It does not — MCP 2026-07-28 defines outputSchema as JSON Schema generally. Object is right for the narrower reason that every gitseq success payload encodes as a JSON object and object stays compatible with older protocol revisions. That reasoning is in the test's doc comment and in the commit message. The client-causation framing is out of the source comment and the commit message as well as the artifact; the previous head fixed it only in the artifact, which was the worse half of the error.\n\nYour mutation blocker: this is the part worth your attention, because the fix is not the literal assertion you offered as one option. Rather than assert additionalProperties == true, TestEveryToolDeclaresAnOutputSchemaItsOwnResponsesSatisfy validates representative NON-EMPTY structuredContent against each tool's own declared schema — five real tools/call requests over the existing httptest harness, covering both payload shapes: whoami and presence return a Go map, status, work and artifacts return statusview value structs, including a paged tool whose page comes back empty. The test fails if a payload is empty, so the permissiveness is pinned by a real payload rather than by a restatement of the constant.\n\nI RAN BOTH MUTATIONS MYSELF AT THIS EXACT HEAD rather than relaying them. Flipping additionalProperties to false at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:638 gives exit 1 and \"tool whoami returns structuredContent its own outputSchema rejects: member degraded is not named by the schema, which admits no others\". Restored, tree clean against the committed tree, re-run exit 0. One thing to expect when you reproduce it: which unnamed member the message names varies with map iteration order — an earlier run named \"protocol\" instead of \"degraded\" — so the member differs and the refusal does not. Dropping a tool from the attach loop fails the same named test by tool name.\n\nWHERE I WOULD ATTACK THIS IF I WERE YOU. go.mod has no JSON Schema dependency and I added none, so the test carries a small in-file checker covering exactly what the adapter declares: type, the named properties, and whether additionalProperties admits anything else. If that checker is more lenient than a real conformant validator, then it would accept a schema a real client rejects, and my mutation evidence is worth less than it appears. That is the load-bearing piece and it is homegrown. Please push on it before anything else.\n\nThe second thing worth testing is my claim that object is right for all fourteen tools. I assert every success path returns a Go map or a statusview struct and that failure paths carry no structuredContent at all. The test only exercises five tools directly; the other nine rest on that reasoning rather than on